Part III: Glossary of Key Terms

Term	Definition
Authentication Hijacking	The theft of a web session from a user who has already been authenticated.
Backdoor	A method (often a hidden script or program) used by a hacker to maintain exclusive, easy access to a compromised system for future use.
Black-Box Testing	A type of pentest performed without any prior knowledge of the target's internal infrastructure or systems.
Clickjacking	An attack that tricks a user into clicking a link or button that performs an action they did not intend, such as a purchase.
CNN (Convolutional Neural Network)	A deep learning model that uses filters (kernels) to extract features from input data, often used in image and text classification.
Cookie Poisoning	The modification or corruption of cookies stored on a user's device to gain unauthorized access.
DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service)	A large-scale attack using many infected devices (zombies/bots) to overwhelm a target service with traffic, making it unavailable to legitimate users.
DOM-based XSS	A cross-site scripting attack where the malicious script is executed by the browser as a result of modifying the Document Object Model (DOM).
Exploit	A piece of code or software that takes advantage of a specific bug or vulnerability to violate system security.
Footprinting	The process of creating a profile or "image" of a target during the reconnaissance phase.
Grey-Box Testing	A pentest where the attacker has partial knowledge of the internal network, simulating an attack by a malicious insider.
Honeypot	A false target or decoy system designed to attract and distract hackers, thereby protecting the real, valuable assets.
Information Gathering	The passive or active collection of data about a target system, including IP ranges, server types, and social media data.
Inside Attack	An attack originating from within the security perimeter of an organization, usually by an individual with privileged access.
LGPD (Lei Geral de Proteção de Dados)	The Brazilian General Data Protection Law, which regulates the processing of personal data to protect privacy.
LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory)	A type of Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) capable of learning long-term dependencies in sequential data, useful for analyzing code strings.
Malware	A general term for malicious software (viruses, worms, trojans, etc.) designed to infiltrate or damage a computer system.
Meterpreter	An advanced payload within Metasploit that provides a powerful, interactive command-line interface on the compromised target.
NoSQL Injection	A vulnerability where an attacker interferes with queries made to non-relational databases (like MongoDB) by injecting malicious syntax or operators.
Phishing	A social engineering technique using deceptive messages (email, SMS) to trick users into revealing sensitive information like login credentials.
Rootkit	A set of software tools used to hide the presence of a hacker and their activities within a compromised system, often by modifying system logs.
Sniffing	The act of capturing and analyzing network traffic to find sensitive information such as passwords or account numbers.
SQL Injection (SQLi)	An attack that inserts malicious SQL code into input fields to manipulate a back-end database, potentially stealing or destroying data.
Staging	In Metasploit, the use of small payloads (stagers) to establish a connection that then downloads a larger, more functional payload.
White-Box Testing	A pentest where the tester has full knowledge of the target's infrastructure, mimicking the access of a network administrator.
XSS (Cross-Site Scripting)	A vulnerability where malicious scripts are injected into web pages viewed by other users, often used to steal session IDs or cookies.
